  "kind": "event",
  "title": "Abd al-Malik's coinage reform",
  "summary": "Between 696 and 698 the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik reformed the caliphate's coinage. Gold dinars were set at 4.25 g with a minimum 96 percent gold, and silver dirhams at 2.97 g. The coins carried Islamic inscriptions and, unlike Byzantine and Sasanian coins, no image of the caliph. Provincial mints struck them, and the governor al-Hajjaj imposed the change in the east.",
